===== 4529 Page 88
1050 AM 
McCaine Cumberland Md  Annapolis Md Apr 13th 1864 
Annapolis Apr 13th 10 A. M. For Gen Sigel . Your
dispatch of yesterday is received and is satis = factory
. A movement up Shenandoah Valley , if necessary 
to make it , will not require much more 
than an escort for the wagon train . I
have directed a regiment of Heavy Artillery to be
sent to you from Baltimore which I do not
see enters into your calculation of forces . In 
addition to this I may in case of an
urgent necessity be able to send you say four
more regiments of Infantry from Washington <insertion>when</insertion> the time
for moving arrives (sig) U. S. Grant Lt Genl


Recd 215 Pm 
Fort Monroe <insertion>1 PM</insertion> Apl 14th 1864
For Secy of War - Can the fifth N. J.
battery be spared to this department from the
defences of Washn ? If so I
should be pleased to have it ordered
here - I do not know but
I should direct this to Gen Grant ,
if so please forward it signed Gen Butler

===== 4530 Page 89
1150 AM
Fort Monroe Va Apr 15th 1864
For Lt Gen Grant . Will you remain long
enough in Washn for Gen Smith or my
Chf of Staff to see you upon business B. F. Butler


2.50 PM
Nashville Tenn Apr 15th 1864
Nashville April 15th noon For Genl Taylor Commissary Genl Washington
. No Commissary has yet reported to me Col Beckwith
is not here . I must have by May first 
near Chattanooga a large amount of beef cattle on the
hoof and each Commissary is making separate contracts utterly useless 
. We cannot supply transportation for cattle they must travel
by land and I may be forced to seize even
breeding cattle in Tennessee to supply meat for we cant
wait our movements for such matters of Economy (sig) W. T. Sherman
Maj Genl


315 PM
S. H Beckwith  Nashville Apr 15th 1864
Nashville April 15th 10 AM For Gen Grant Washn
I have dispatch from Little Rock of April
10th giving dates from Gen Steele of April 7th
at Camden . He had considerable skirmishing 
in all which he was successful and had
halted and sent back to Pine Bluff for
provisions and ammunition . It seems to me 
his movement is very slow and he may
be so late in reaching Red River as to
keep Gen Banks and A J Smith away behind behind
time (sig) W. T. Sherman Maj Genl

===== 4676 Page 235
620 pm  Ft Monroe May 31st 1864
530 PM
Genl S Williams Asst Adjt Genl - Army Potomac 
, I have just had sent to me by
Lt Col Biggs QM at this post an order
of Gen Halleck to him that all Ponton bridging that
arrives here shall be sent to Butler . I
therefore propose when the Pontons arrive , as is
not the case yet , To send some one 
hundred fifty or two hundred men with them to
Butler and retain here the balance of the Brigade
with the special material trains etc for your further
orders , as I presume to what you would 
desire - - - H Benham by


630 pm  Fortress Monroe May 31
Br Genl Taylor Comy Genl ---- I am 
prepared to send two millions rations and one thousand
Head of cattle to the White House . shall
I send them ---- M P Small Lt Col
&C S 630 pm

===== 4601 Page 160
4.30 P. M
Ft Monroe Va May 6th 64
Off City Point 9 pm May fifth via Ft Monroe 130 am
May 6th for Gen Grant Washn ---- we have
seized Wilsons Wharf landing a Brigade (wilds Colored
troops there ---- Fort Powhattan landing two Regt
of same brigade ---- have landed at City Point
Hinks Div of Colored troops remaining"  Brigade & &
battery - Remainder of both Eighteenth and 10th
Army Corps are now being landed at Bermuda
hundreds above the appo - Mattox ---- no opposition
thus far apparently a complete surprise both army
corps left Yorktown during last night ---- Monitors
all over the bar at Harrisons landing and
above City Point the operation of the fleet
have been conducted today with energy and success
---- Genls Smith & Gen Gillmore are pushing the
landing of their men Genl Graham with the
army Gunboats led the advance during the night
capturing the signal stations of the rebels ----
Col West with 18 hundred cavalry made demonstrations
from Williamsburg yesterday morning Genl Kautz left
Suffolk this morning with three thousand cavalry
for the services indicated in conference with
the Lt General ---- The New York Flag
of truce boat was found lying at
the wharf with four hundred Rebel prisoners
which she had not had time to
deliver ---- she went the yesterday morning
---- We are landing the troops during
the night a hazardous service in face
of the enemy BF Butler 
Maj Gen

===== 2917 Page 27
Berkley Aug 12th 11 P. M. 
Genl Halleck Your dispatch of noon today received
it is positively the fact that no 
more men could be embarked hence than 
have gone and that no unnecessary delay
has occurred before your orders were received.
Col Ingalls directed all available vessels to
come from Ft Monroe officers have been sent
to take personal direction. Have heard nothing
here of Burnsides fleet. There are some 
vessels at Ft Monroe such as Atlantic &
Illinois which draw too much to come
here. Hospital accommodations exhausted this side of
N York. propose filling Atlantic & Illinois
with serious cases for N York &
to Encamp slight cases for the
present at Ft Monroe in this way
can probably get off the thirty 
four hundred sick still on hand
by day after tomorrow night. I 
am sure that you have been
misinformed as to availability of vessels
on hand - we cannot use heavily
(over) 

